This is a simulation of an XYZBCWY 5 axis bridge mill with a
tilting head (B, C) and a quill (W).

The vismach model draws the quill: positive W slides it out of the
head along the tool axis, negative W retracts it.  With the default
TCP kinematics a W word does not move the tool tip in world
coordinates, the kinematics folds the quill motion into the XYZ
joints so the tip stays put, the same way a B or C word reorients
the head around a fixed tip.  To actually drill with the quill,
use a point-to-point move (g53.5 or g53.7).  The demo programs
below show the various ways to skin this cat, including the
historical one and why it is a hack.

Demo programs:

 g532-fused-orient-move.ngc -- drill a sphere pattern with tilted work
                             planes (g68.2), using g53.2 to solve each
                             orientation without moving (STAY), then
                             one fused g0 that turns the head and
                             travels to the hole at the same time
                             (TCP motion)
 g536-orient-then-move.ngc -- the same pattern with g53.6 (Heidenhain
                             MOVE style): reorient about the fixed
                             tip, then travel in the tilted plane
 g535-quill.ngc ------------- the same pattern drilled with the quill
                             itself: B and C words aim the head, no
                             tilted work planes, and each hole is
                             drilled by the quill alone with a
                             point-to-point move (g53.5 reads its
                             axis words as joints, so only the quill
                             extends; g53.7 j5=.. names the joint by
                             number and does the same)
 g121-switch-kins.ngc ------- the same pattern with the quill stroke
                             done by a g12.1 p1 / g13.1 kinematics
                             switch; the least recommended of the
                             quill demos, kept to demonstrate
                             G12.1/G13.1
 w-historical.ngc ----------- the historical demo program, unchanged:
                             it "drills" with W words, using W as a
                             tool length substitute, a LinuxCNC-local
                             hack that never was a widely known
                             convention and only looked right because
                             the quill was not drawn.  It runs with
                             the quill drawing locked (m64 p0), which
                             reveals the hack: the head does the same
                             motion it always did, the drawn tool tip
                             plunges, the real tip stays put.  Kept
                             to show why the old trick worked; for
                             real drilling use the methods above

The tool table provides tool 100 (length 100).  Load it with
t100m6g43 so the vismach tool and the kinematics pivot length
(pivotsum: 250 + tool length) match.

Example MDI:

 1) $ linuxcnc 5axis.ini
 2) F1 ---------- Estop off
    F2 ---------- Machine on
    CTRL-HOME --- home all
    F5 ---------- MDI tab
 3) orient vismach gui as required
 4) g0w10   ; extend quill
 5) g43h100 ; tool offset (100)
 6) g0b45   ; tilt 45 deg wrt z
 7) g0c30   ; rotate 30 deg in xy
 8) g0w-10  ; retract quill, head compensates, tip stays
 9) g0w10   ; extend quill again
10) etc

Note: Motion for the W coordinate is incorporated
into the motions of the joints used for X,Y,Z
motion but a corresponding joint.5 is needed to
correctly display W values.

The joint.5.motor-pos-cmd should be looped to the
joint.5.motor-pos-fb and joint.5 should use immediate
homimg (HOME_SEARCH_VEL = 0).

The 5axis.ini illustrates how to use multiple (2)
joints for the axis y coordinate.

Configuration for XYZBCWY (7 joints total)
J0: X
J1: Y
J2: Z
J3: B
J4: C
J5: W
J6: Y
